<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exam review</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that we define a function
        <md>
          F(x) = \int_0^x \frac{\sin(t)}t~dt
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>F'(2)</m>.  Is <m>F</m> increasing or decreasing at
          <m>x=2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Since <m>F'(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}x</m>, we have
          <m>F'(2)=\frac{\sin(2)}{2} > 0</m> so <m>F</m> is
          increasing.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>F''(2)</m>.  Is <m>F</m> concave up or down at
          <m>x=2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Since <m>F'(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}{x}</m>, it follows that
          <md>
            F''(x) = \frac{x\cos(x)-\sin(x)}{x^2}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            F''(x) = \frac{2\cos(2)-\sin(2)}{4} =-0.436
          </md>
          so the graph of <m>F</m> is concave down.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the first three critical points of <m>F</m> to the
          right of <m>x=0</m> and determine whether they are relative
          maxima or minima.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          <m>F'(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}{x} = 0</m> at <m>x=\pi,2\pi,3\pi</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the value of <m>F</m> at the first critical point
          that you found.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Using Desmos, <m>F(\pi)=\int_0^\pi\frac{\sin(t)}{t}~dt =
          1.85</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find antiderivatives of the following functions.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds\int \frac{3x}{4x^2+1}~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Let's try the substitution <m>u=4x^2+1</m> so that <m>du =
          8x~dx</m>.  Then
          <md>
            \int \frac{3x}{4x^2+1}~dx = \frac38\int \frac1u~du =
            \frac38 \ln|u|+C = \frac38\ln|4x^2+1|+C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds\int \frac{3x}{(4x^2+1)^3}~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          With the same substitution <m>u=4x^2+1</m>,
          <md>
            \int \frac{3x}{(4x^2+1)^3}~dx = \frac38\int \frac1u^3~du =
            -\frac3{16} \frac1{u^2}+C = -\frac3{16}\frac{1}{(4x^2+1)^2}+C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds\int \arctan(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          This isn't on the table of antiderivatives, and a
          substitution isn't going to help.  Let's try integration by
          parts where we differentiate <m>\arctan(x)</m>:
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u=\arctan(x) \amp du=\frac1{1+x^2}~dx \\
            dv=dx \amp v = x \\
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int \arctan(x)~dx \amp= x\arctan(x) -
              \int\frac{x}{1+x^2}~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = x\arctan(x) - \frac12\ln|1+x^2|+C
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds\int 4xe^{4x}~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Let's try integration by parts:
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u=x \amp du=dx \\
            dv = 4e^{4x}~dx \amp v= e^{4x}~dx 
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int 4xe^{4x}~dx \amp= xe^{4x} - 
              \int e^{4x}~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int 4xe^{4x}~dx \amp= xe^{4x} - 
              \frac14 e^{4x} + C
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds\int_1^3 x\sin(2x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          Let's try integration by parts:
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u=x \amp du=dx \\
            dv = \sin(2x)~dx \amp v= -\frac12\cos(2x)~dx 
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int x\sin(2x)~dx \amp= -\frac12
              x\cos(2x)+\frac12\int\cos(2x)~dx 
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -\frac12
              x\cos(2x)+\frac14 \sin(2x)+C
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Now
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int_1^3 x\sin(2x)~dx \amp =
              \left(-\frac12x\cos(2x)+\frac14\sin(2x)\right)|^3_1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp =-\frac12(3\cos(6)-\cos(2)) +
              \frac14(\sin(6)-\sin(2))
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -1.95
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
        <p>
          Shown on the right is the graph of the function
          <md>f(x)=x^3-3x^2-x+3</md>.
          Find the area of the shaded region.
        </p>
        <image>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="19-area">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/19-area.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <solution component="19">
      <p>
        We could look at the area under the graph between <m>-1\leq x
        \leq 0</m> and then add in the area of the triangle.  So
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A \amp = \int_{-1}^0 (x^3-3x^2-x+3)~dx + \frac12 2\cdot 2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \left(\frac14x^4-x^3-\frac12x^2+3x\right)\bigg|_{-1}^0 + 2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{17}4
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider a tank that is obtained by spinning the graph
        <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> between <m>0\leq x \leq 4</m> about the
        vertical axis.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the volume of the water tank.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          We will slice horizontal so that the cross sectional radius
          is <m>x=y^2</m>.  Then <m>A(y)=\pi y^4</m> and <m>dV=\pi
          y^4~dy</m>.  Then
          <md>
            V = \int_0^2 \pi y^4~dy = \frac{32\pi}{5}
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the tank is filled with a fluid whose density is given by
          <m>\rho(y)=10-y</m>, find the mass of fluid.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          <m>dm = \rho(y)~dV = \pi(10-y)y^4~dy</m> so
          <md>
            V = \int_0^2 \pi (10-y)y^4~dy = \pi\int_0^2 (10y^4-y^5)~dy
            = \pi(2\cdot 2^5 - \frac16 2^6) = \frac{160\pi}{3}
          </md>.          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume the dimensions are in meters and the density is in
          kilograms per cubic meter.
          Find the amount of work required to pump all of the fluid to
          the a location one meter above the top of the tank.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          A slice at location <m>y</m> needs to be raised to
          <m>y=3</m> so the distance is <m>3-y</m>.  Therefore,
          <m>dW = (3-y)g~dm = \pi g(3-y)(10-y)y^4~dy</m> so that
          <md>
            W = \int_0^2 \pi g(3-y)(10-y)y^4~dy = 2205
          </md>
          Joules.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you siphon off half of the volume, how deep is the fluid
          remaining?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="19">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              V \amp = \int_0^H \pi y^4~dy = \frac12\frac{32\pi}{5}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \frac15H^5 \amp = \frac{16}{5}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              H \amp = (16)^{1/5} = 1.74
            </mrow>
          </md>
          meters.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
